{{fbdoc item="title" value="Compiler source tree"}}

The compiler lives in ##src/compiler##. Its ##.bas## files implement the compiler itself; its ##.bi## files describe internal interfaces. The subsystem README (##src/compiler/README.md##) has the detailed ownership and lifetime contracts. This page gives a reading route through those modules.

%%
compiler/
    driver/                 Invocation, options, paths, tools and final linking
        platforms/          Policy for the user's compilation target
    core/                   Compiler environment and source-module lifecycle
    diagnostics/            Errors, warnings and source context
    lexer/                  Source characters, tokens and locations
    preprocessor/           Macros, conditional compilation and directives
    parser/
        declarations/       Variables, types, constants and initializers
        expressions/        Precedence, values, calls and member access
        procedures/         Signatures, arguments and procedure bodies
        statements/         Assignment and compound control flow
        intrinsics/         Statements/functions with special BASIC syntax
    symbols/                Names, visibility, types, layouts and ABI metadata
    ast/
        nodes/              Typed operation builders and lowering
        optimize/           Tree simplification and vectorization
    runtime/                Runtime declarations and typed call builders
    backend/                IR interface and backend dispatch
        c/ llvm/ gas64/ x86/ debug/
    support/                Allocation, containers, strings and numeric helpers
    platform/               Compiler-host implementation policies
    tooling/                Compiler-derived semantic observations and export
%%

{{fbdoc item="section" value="Start at the phase that owns the behavior"}}

	- Invocation options, files and tools: ##src/compiler/driver/fbc.bas## and the driver's phase modules.
	- Source-module initialization and compilation: ##src/compiler/core/fb.bas##.
	- Source text and tokens: ##src/compiler/lexer/lex.bas##, then ##src/compiler/preprocessor/pp.bas##.
	- Accepted and rejected syntax: the corresponding ##parser## family, with its positive and negative tests.
	- Name lookup and type layout: ##symbols##, with ##src/compiler/symbols/symb.bi## as the shared contract.
	- Conversions, cleanup and runtime calls: ##ast/nodes## and the relevant ##runtime/rtl-*.bas## module.
	- Emitted instructions and ABI forms: ##src/compiler/backend/ir.bi##, then the selected backend.
	- Semantic sidecar facts: ##tooling## and its observation hooks in the owning compiler phase.

The parser is recursive descent. A parser module may describe the common grammar in comments, but its branches, dialect checks and recovery behavior determine what is accepted. An accepted parse is only part of compilation: symbol and AST checks still enforce types, argument modes and lifetime rules.

{{fbdoc item="section" value="Trace PRINT from syntax to runtime"}}

For a string-valued ##PRINT## argument, the useful path is:

	1) ##src/compiler/parser/intrinsics/parser-quirk-file.bas## implements ##cPrintStmt()##. It parses the destination, expressions and formatting separators and asks the runtime lowering layer to build calls.
	2) ##src/compiler/runtime/rtl-print.bas## describes printing entry points and chooses a typed operation for the expression. The string family includes the runtime symbol ##fb_PrintString##.
	3) The resulting AST call passes through the selected backend. That backend emits the target's call sequence rather than printing anything itself.
	4) At program execution, ##src/rtlib/console/io_print.c## implements ##fb_PrintString()##. It handles the string descriptor, formatting and temporary-string cleanup and dispatches through the runtime's file output machinery.

This separation matters when fixing a bug. Incorrect parsing of a separator belongs in the parser; a wrong parameter declaration belongs in the call builder; an output or temporary-lifetime bug belongs in the runtime. A graphics-mode output path may also involve the runtime hooks described in the [[DevSourceRuntime|runtime guide]].

{{fbdoc item="section" value="Keep host policy separate from target code generation"}}

##driver/platforms## describes how to build programs for supported targets. ##platform## supplies implementations needed by the machine running the compiler. For example, compiler-host numeric representation is a different question from the target program's calling convention.

The C, LLVM and assembly backends implement a common IR interface, but have different emission and ABI details. Check the affected backend's implementation and interoperability tests before changing parameter layout, aggregate passing, inline assembly or debug emission. A fix in one emitter does not automatically cover the others.

##tooling## records facts selected by the compiler. Its observers must obtain identities, types and source ranges before the corresponding compiler storage is released. It must not reparse text to invent a competing interpretation. The semantic model contract (##doc/compiler-semantic-model.md##) describes the exported format separately from these implementation boundaries.

{{fbdoc item="section" value="Adding or moving a compiler module"}}

##mk/compiler-sources.mk## lists source groups explicitly. Compiler objects and bootstrap emission retain flat module names, so selected modules must have unique basenames. The host policy can replace a generic module by its basename; an unrelated duplicate is an error.

Put a new module with the subsystem that owns its state. Name includes from the compiler source root, preserve the shared interfaces, and update the source group list when adding a directory. Keep driver-private state out of parser and backend modules. Shared compiler contexts are used serially within a process; parallel Make jobs run separate compiler processes.

##make compiler-structure-test## checks the maintained source map and include contracts. Behavior changes also need a regression in the relevant language, runtime or backend suite. See [[DevSourceTools|tools and tests]].


